ENTRY 11905 {"id":11905,"kind":"pi.tool-result","messages":[{"role":"toolResult","text":"./examples/bug-hunt-2026-11-16/raw/5-deploy-and-scripts.stream:11:ENTRY 9587 {\"id\":9587,\"kind\":\"pi.tool-result\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"toolResult\",\"text\":\"====\\n     1\\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n     2\\t# Fill ConfigMap `onecli-ca` in pi-durable-poc with the OneCLI gateway CA. The orchestrator trusts it for the\\n     3\\t# certificates the gateway presents for model hosts. The CA is public (no auth): OneCLI v2 serves it at\\n     4\\t# GET :10256/v1/gateway/ca (v1.45 served it on :10254). The CA persists in OneCLI's data volume, so this runs once\\n     5\\t# and again only if OneCLI's data is recreated. Read-only against OneCLI. Usage: deploy/fetch-onecli-ca.sh\\n     6\\tset -euo pipefail\\n     7\\t\\n     8\\tctx=${KUBE_CONTEXT:-}\\n     9\\tns=${NAMESPACE:-pi-durable-poc}\\n    10\\twork=$(mktemp -d)\\n    11\\tpf_pid=\\n    12\\tcleanup() {\\n    13\\t  [[ -z $pf_pid ]] || kill \\\"$pf_pid\\\" 2>/dev/null || true\\n    14\\t  rm -rf \\\"$work\\\"\\n    15\\t}\\n    16\\ttrap cleanup EXIT\\n    17\\t\\n    18\\t# A free local port; the API is cluster-internal, so go through a port-forward.\\n    19\\tport=$(python3 -c 'import socket; s=socket.socket(); s.bind((\\\"127.0.0.1\\\",0)); print(s.getsockname()[1])')\\n    20\\tkubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n onecli port-forward svc/onecli \\\"$port:10256\\\" >/dev/null 2>&1 &\\n    21\\tpf_pid=$!\\n    22\\tfor _ in $(seq 1 20); do\\n    23\\t  curl -fsS -m 3 -o \\\"$work/ca.pem\\\" \\\"http://127.0.0.1:$port/v1/gateway/ca\\\" 2>/dev/null && break\\n    24\\t  sleep 1\\n    25\\tdone\\n    26\\t[[ -s $work/ca.pem ]] || { echo \\\"could not fetch the CA from onecli:10256/v1/gateway/ca\\\" >&2; exit 1; }\\n    27\\t# Refuse anything that is not a certificate (an error page, a login redirect).\\n    28\\topenssl x509 -in \\\"$work/ca.pem\\\" -noout -subject -fingerprint -sha256 >&2\\n    29\\tkubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" create configmap onecli-ca --from-file=ca.pem=\\\"$work/ca.pem\\\" \\\\\\n    30\\t  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" apply -f - >/dev/null\\n    31\\techo \\\"configmap onecli-ca updated\\\"\\n====\\n     1\\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n     2\\t# Create secret api-auth in pi-durable-poc: key `tokens` holds one `owner:token` line per API owner.\\n     3\\t# Never commit or print its content. Idempotent: an existing secret is left alone (set ROTATE=1 to replace it;\\n     4\\t# running orchestrator and ingest pods pick the new value up on their next restart).\\n     5\\t# Owners: alice and bob, so the e2e suite can test \\\"wrong owner\\\". Real owners replace these when OIDC lands.\\n     6\\tset -euo pipefail\\n     7\\tctx=${KUBE_CONTEXT:-} # empty when run in-cluster (e2e driver Job): ServiceAccount config\\n     8\\tns=${NAMESPACE:-pi-durable-poc}\\n     9\\tif [[ ${ROTATE:-0} != 1 ]] && kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" get secret api-auth >/dev/null 2>&1; then\\n    10\\t  echo \\\"secret api-auth already exists (ROTATE=1 to replace)\\\"\\n    11\\t  exit 0\\n    12\\tfi\\n    13\\ttokens=$(printf 'alice:%s\\\\nbob:%s\\\\n' \\\"$(openssl rand -hex 32)\\\" \\\"$(openssl rand -hex 32)\\\")\\n    14\\tkubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" create secret generic api-auth \\\\\\n    15\\t  --from-literal=tokens=\\\"$tokens\\\" \\\\\\n    16\\t  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" apply -f - >/dev/null\\n    17\\techo \\\"secret api-auth created\\\"\\n====\\n     1\\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n     2\\t# Create secret sandboxd-auth in pi-durable-poc with one key, `master`: the key the orchestrator derives every\\n     3\\t# sandbox's token from (token = HMAC-SHA256(master, claimName), packages/sandbox-env/src/token.ts). Never commit or\\n     4\\t# print its value. A sandbox only ever gets the sha256 of its own token (SandboxClaim env), never the master.\\n     5\\t# Idempotent: an existing master is kept. ROTATE=1 replaces it; every existing claim then has to be recreated\\n     6\\t# (docs/decisions/0001-per-sandbox-sandboxd-token.md). Keys from the earlier shared-token scheme (`token`,\\n     7\\t# `token-sha256`) are removed.\\n     8\\tset -euo pipefail\\n     9\\tctx=${KUBE_CONTEXT:-}\\n    10\\tns=${NAMESPACE:-pi-durable-poc}\\n    11\\tk() { kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" \\\"$@\\\"; }\\n    12\\t\\n    13\\texists=0\\n    14\\tif k get secret sandboxd-auth >/dev/null 2>&1; then\\n    15\\t  exists=1\\n    16\\t  if [[ -n $(k get secret sandboxd-auth -o jsonpath='{.data.master}') && ${ROTATE:-0} != 1 ]]; then\\n    17\\t    echo \\\"sandboxd-auth: master exists, kept (ROTATE=1 to replace)\\\"\\n    18\\t    exit 0\\n    19\\t  fi\\n    20\\tfi\\n    21\\tmaster=$(openssl rand -hex 32)\\n    22\\tmanifest=$(k create secret generic sandboxd-auth --from-literal=master=\\\"$master\\\" --dry-run=client -o yaml)\\n    23\\t# replace swaps the whole data map, which also drops the legacy keys.\\n    24\\tif [[ $exists == 1 ]]; then k replace -f - <<<\\\"$manifest\\\" >/dev/null; else k create -f - <<<\\\"$manifest\\\" >/dev/null; fi\\n    25\\techo \\\"sandboxd-auth: master written\\\"\\n====\\n     1\\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n     2\\t# Apply the OneCLI v2 host allowlist (docs/onecli-host-rules.md): one `allow` org rule per host, then `block *`.\\n     3\\t# Default is a dry run that only prints the plan and makes no request. --apply writes through the OneCLI API.\\n     4\\t#\\n     5\\t#   deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh            # plan only\\n     6\\t#   deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh --apply    # create the missing rules, block last\\n     7\\t#\\n     8\\t# The admin key (`oc_...`) is read from Secret secret-store/pi-onecli-admin, key api-key, with kubectl (read only), or\\n     9\\t# from ONECLI_API_KEY. It is passed to curl through a config on stdin: never printed, never on a command line.\\n    10\\t# ONECLI_URL is the API base (required, e.g. https://onecli.example.com; or http://localhost:10256 after\\n    11\\t# `kubectl -n onecli port-forward svc/onecli 10256`). KUBE_CONTEXT defaults to the current kubectl context.\\n    12\\t#\\n    13\\t# Rules are matched by name (`pi-allow-<host>`, `pi-block-all`), so a rerun only adds what is missing. Order matters:\\n    14\\t# the block rule must come after every allow rule. If an allow rule has to be added while `pi-block-all` exists, the\\n    15\\t# block rule is deleted and recreated last. Rules written here are live at once and change what every OneCLI agent\\n    16\\t# can reach, so run this only when the hosts below are complete for the work in flight.\\n    17\\tset -euo pipefail\\n    18\\t\\n    19\\tapply=0\\n    20\\t[[ ${1:-} == --apply ]] && apply=1\\n    21\\tbase=${ONECLI_URL:?set ONECLI_URL to the OneCLI API base, e.g. https://onecli.example.com}\\n    22\\tctx=${KUBE_CONTEXT:-}\\n    23\\trules_path=/v1/org/policy/rules\\n    24\\t\\n    25\\t# Keep in step with deploy/onecli-policies/egress.yaml (the pod's FQDN allowlist).\\n    26\\thosts=(\\n    27\\t  chatgpt.com '*.chatgpt.com' auth.openai.com api.openai.com openrouter.ai\\n    28\\t  github.com api.github.com codeload.github.com objects.githubusercontent.com\\n    29\\t  registry.npmjs.org pypi.org files.pythonhosted.org\\n    30\\t)\\n    31\\tblock_name=pi-block-all\\n    32\\t\\n    33\\trule_name() { printf 'pi-allow-%s' \\\"${1//\\\\*./wild-}\\\"; }\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\tkey=${ONECLI_API_KEY:-}\\n    36\\tif [[ -z $key ]]; then\\n    37\\t  key=$(kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n secret-store get secret pi-onecli-admin -o jsonpath='{.data.api-key}' | base64 -d)\\n    38\\tfi\\n    39\\t[[ -n $key ]] || { echo \\\"no admin key (secret-store/pi-onecli-admin api-key or ONECLI_API_KEY)\\\" >&2; exit 1; }\\n    40\\t\\n    41\\tapi() { # method path [json body]: prints the response body, fails on HTTP >= 400\\n    42\\t  local method=$1 path=$2 body=${3:-}\\n    43\\t  local args=(-sS --fail-with-body -X \\\"$method\\\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -K - \\\"$base$path\\\")\\n    44\\t  [[ -n $body ]] && args+=(--data \\\"$body\\\")\\n    45\\t  printf 'header = \\\"Authorization: Bearer %s\\\"\\\\n' \\\"$key\\\" | curl \\\"${args[@]}\\\"\\n    46\\t}\\n    47\\trule_json() { # name action host\\n    48\\t  jq -nc --arg n \\\"$1\\\" --arg a \\\"$2\\\" --arg h \\\"$3\\\" '{name:$n, action:$a, targets:[{kind:\\\"network\\\", hostPattern:$h}]}'\\n    49\\t}\\n    50\\t\\n    51\\tif [[ $apply -eq 1 ]]; then\\n    52\\t  existing=$(api GET \\\"$rules_path\\\")\\n    53\\telse\\n    54\\t  existing='[]'\\n    55\\t  echo \\\"dry run: not contacting OneCLI, so every rule is shown as missing. Use --apply to write.\\\"\\n    56\\tfi\\n    57\\t# The list is a bare array on v2.20.0; the wrapped forms are accepted in case a later version changes it.\\n    58\\tnames=$(jq -r '(if type==\\\"array\\\" then . else (.rules // .data // []) end)[].name' <<<\\\"$existing\\\")\\n    59\\tblock_id=$(jq -r --arg n \\\"$block_name\\\" '(if type==\\\"array\\\" then . else (.rules // .data // []) end)[] | select(.name==$n) | .id' <<<\\\"$existing\\\" | head -1)\\n    60\\thas() { grep -qxF -- \\\"$1\\\" <<<\\\"$names\\\"; }\\n    61\\t\\n    62\\tmissing=()\\n    63\\tfor h in \\\"${hosts[@]}\\\"; do has \\\"$(rule_name \\\"$h\\\")\\\" || missing+=(\\\"$h\\\"); done\\n    64\\t\\n    65\\tif [[ -n $block_id && ${#missing[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then\\n    66\\t  echo \\\"plan: delete $block_name ($block_id) so it can be recreated after the new allow rules\\\"\\n    67\\t  [[ $apply -eq 1 ]] && api DELETE \\\"$rules_path/$block_id\\\" >/dev/null\\n    68\\t  block_id=\\\"\\\"\\n    69\\tfi\\n    70\\tfor h in ${missing[@]+\\\"${missing[@]}\\\"}; do\\n    71\\t  echo \\\"plan: allow $h\\\"\\n    72\\t  [[ $apply -eq 1 ]] && api POST \\\"$rules_path\\\" \\\"$(rule_json \\\"$(rule_name \\\"$h\\\")\\\" allow \\\"$h\\\")\\\" >/dev/null\\n    73\\tdone\\n    74\\tif [[ -z $block_id ]]; then\\n    75\\t  echo \\\"plan: block * (last)\\\"\\n    76\\t  [[ $apply -eq 1 ]] && api POST \\\"$rules_path\\\" \\\"$(rule_json \\\"$block_name\\\" block '*')\\\" >/dev/null\\n    77\\tfi\\n    78\\t[[ $apply -eq 1 ]] && echo \\\"done: ${#hosts[@]} allow rules, then block *. Verify with docs/onecli-host-rules.md.\\\" || true\\n====\\n     1\\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n     2\\t# Bundle the orchestrator and copy it onto the orchestrator-code PVC, so the stock node image can run it and\\n     3\\t# a pod restart keeps the code. No image is built or pushed. The orchestrator Deployment (if present) is\\n     4\\t# scaled to 0 while the volume is written, because a ReadWriteOnce volume is mounted by one node at a time.\\n     5\\t# Usage: deploy/push-code.sh [deployment...]   (default: pi-orchestrator; scaled back to 1 afterwards)\\n     6\\tset -euo pipefail\\n     7\\t\\n     8\\tctx=${KUBE_CONTEXT:-}\\n     9\\tns=${NAMESPACE:-pi-durable-poc}\\n    10\\there=$(cd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$0\\\")\\\" && pwd)\\n    11\\troot=$(cd \\\"$here/..\\\" && pwd)\\n    12\\twork=$(mktemp -d)\\n    13\\tdeployments=(\\\"$@\\\")\\n    14\\t[[ ${#deployments[@]} -gt 0 ]] || deployments=(pi-orchestrator)\\n    15\\tk() { kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" \\\"$@\\\"; }\\n    16\\ttrap 'rm -rf \\\"$work\\\"; k delete pod orchestrator-code-loader --ignore-not-found --wait=false >/dev/null 2>&1 || true' EXIT\\n    17\\t\\n    18\\t(cd \\\"$root\\\" && pnpm install --frozen-lockfile >/dev/null && pnpm --filter \\\"@pi-durable-k8s/orchestrator...\\\" build >/dev/null)\\n    19\\t(cd \\\"$root\\\" && pnpm --filter @pi-durable-k8s/orchestrator deploy --prod \\\"$work/app\\\" >/dev/null)\\n    20\\tCOPYFILE_DISABLE=1 tar --no-xattrs -czf \\\"$work/app.tgz\\\" -C \\\"$work/app\\\" .\\n    21\\t\\n    22\\tk apply -f \\\"$here/orchestrator-code-pvc.yaml\\\" >/dev/null\\n    23\\tfor d in \\\"${deployments[@]}\\\"; do\\n    24\\t  k get deployment \\\"$d\\\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && k scale deployment \\\"$d\\\" --replicas=0 >/dev/null\\n    25\\tdone\\n    26\\tfor d in \\\"${deployments[@]}\\\"; do\\n    27\\t  k wait --for=delete pod -l \\\"app.kubernetes.io/instance=$d\\\" --timeout=120s >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\\n    28\\tdone\\n    29\\t\\n    30\\tk delete pod orchestrator-code-loader --ignore-not-found --wait=true >/dev/null\\n    31\\tk apply -f - >/dev/null <<EOF\\n    32\\tapiVersion: v1\\n    33\\tkind: Pod\\n    34\\tmetadata:\\n    35\\t  name: orchestrator-code-loader\\n    36\\tspec:\\n    37\\t  restartPolicy: Never\\n    38\\t  securityContext: { runAsNonRoot: true, runAsUser: 1000, runAsGroup: 1000, fsGroup: 1000, seccompProfile: { type: RuntimeDefault } }\\n    39\\t  containers:\\n    40\\t    - name: loader\\n    41\\t      image: node:22-bookworm-slim\\n    42\\t      command: [sleep, \\\"600\\\"]\\n    43\\t      securityContext: { allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, capabilities: { drop: [ALL] } }\\n    44\\t      resources:\\n    45\\t        requests: { cpu: 10m, memory: 32Mi }\\n    46\\t        limits: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi }\\n    47\\t      volumeMounts: [{ name: code, mountPath: /code }]\\n    48\\t  volumes:\\n    49\\t    - name: code\\n    50\\t      persistentVolumeClaim: { claimName: orchestrator-code }\\n    51\\tEOF\\n    52\\tk wait --for=condition=Ready pod/orchestrator-code-loader --timeout=240s >/dev/null\\n    53\\tk cp \\\"$work/app.tgz\\\" orchestrator-code-loader:/tmp/app.tgz >/dev/null\\n    54\\tk exec orchestrator-code-loader -- sh -c 'rm -rf /code/app && mkdir -p /code/app && tar xzf /tmp/app.tgz -C /code/app && ls /code/app/dist/main.js >/dev/null'\\n    55\\tk delete pod orchestrator-code-loader --wait=true >/dev/null\\n    56\\t\\n    57\\tfor d in \\\"${deployments[@]}\\\"; do\\n    58\\t  k get deployment \\\"$d\\\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && k scale deployment \\\"$d\\\" --replicas=1 >/dev/null\\n    59\\tdone\\n    60\\techo \\\"orchestrator code pushed to PVC orchestrator-code\\\"\\n====\\n     1\\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n     2\\t# Bundle ingest (with its workspace dependency session-core) into ConfigMap `ingest-code`, which the\\n     3\\t# pi-ingest Deployment unpacks in an initContainer. No image is built or pushed. Restarts the pods when the\\n     4\\t# bundle changed. Usage: deploy/push-ingest.sh\\n     5\\tset -euo pipefail\\n     6\\t\\n     7\\tctx=${KUBE_CONTEXT:-}\\n     8\\tns=${NAMESPACE:-pi-durable-poc}\\n     9\\there=$(cd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$0\\\")\\\" && pwd)\\n    10\\troot=$(cd \\\"$here/..\\\" && pwd)\\n    11\\twork=$(mktemp -d)\\n    12\\tk() { kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" \\\"$@\\\"; }\\n    13\\ttrap 'rm -rf \\\"$work\\\"' EXIT\\n    14\\t\\n    15\\t(cd \\\"$root\\\" && pnpm install --frozen-lockfile >/dev/null && pnpm --filter @pi-durable-k8s/session-core --filter @pi-durable-k8s/ingest build >/dev/null)\\n    16\\t(cd \\\"$root\\\" && pnpm --filter @pi-durable-k8s/ingest deploy --prod \\\"$work/app\\\" >/dev/null)\\n    17\\tCOPYFILE_DISABLE=1 tar --no-xattrs -czf \\\"$work/app.tgz\\\" -C \\\"$work/app\\\" .\\n    18\\tsize=$(wc -c <\\\"$work/app.tgz\\\" | tr -d ' ')\\n    19\\tif [[ $size -gt 900000 ]]; then\\n    20\\t  echo \\\"ingest bundle is $size bytes; a ConfigMap holds 1 MiB. Switch to a code PVC (see deploy/ingest.yaml).\\\" >&2\\n    21\\t  exit 1\\n    22\\tfi\\n    23\\t\\n    24\\tsum=$(shasum -a 256 \\\"$work/app.tgz\\\" | awk '{print $1}')\\n    25\\tcurrent=$(k get configmap ingest-code -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.bundle-sha256}' 2>/dev/null || true)\\n    26\\tif [[ $current == \\\"$sum\\\" ]]; then\\n    27\\t  echo \\\"ingest bundle unchanged\\\"\\n    28\\t  exit 0\\n    29\\tfi\\n    30\\tk create configmap ingest-code --from-file=app.tgz=\\\"$work/app.tgz\\\" --dry-run=client -o yaml |\\n    31\\t  k annotate --local -f - \\\"bundle-sha256=$sum\\\" -o yaml | k apply -f - >/dev/null\\n    32\\tif k get deployment pi-ingest >/dev/null 2>&1; then\\n    33\\t  k rollout restart deployment/pi-ingest >/dev/null\\n    34\\tfi\\n    35\\techo \\\"ingest bundle pushed ($size bytes)\\\"\\n\",\"isError\":false}]}\n./examples/bug-hunt-2026-11-16/raw/5-deploy-and-scripts.stream:13:ENTRY 9591 {\"id\":9591,\"kind\":\"pi.tool-result\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"toolResult\",\"text\":\"apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1\\nkind: Kustomization\\nnamespace: pi-durable-poc\\nresources:\\n  - namespace.yaml\\n  - sandbox-template.yaml\\n  - sandbox-warmpool.yaml\\n  - networkpolicy.yaml\\n  - networkpolicy-ingest.yaml\\n  - networkpolicy-orchestrator.yaml\\n  - networkpolicy-postgres.yaml\\n  - networkpolicy-e2e.yaml\\n  - postgres.yaml\\n  - orchestrator-rbac.yaml\\n  - orchestrator-code-pvc.yaml\\n  - orchestrator-service.yaml\\n  - model-config.yaml\\n  - onecli-externalsecret.yaml\\n  - repo-externalsecrets.yaml\\n  - orchestrator.yaml\\n  - ingest.yaml\\n# Secrets `sandboxd-auth` and `api-auth` are NOT here: create them with deploy/gen-secret.sh and\\n# deploy/gen-api-secret.sh. ConfigMap `ingest-code` is filled by deploy/push-ingest.sh.\\n# Secret `pi-onecli` comes from onecli-externalsecret.yaml; ConfigMap `onecli-ca` from deploy/fetch-onecli-ca.sh.\\n# Secret `pi-pg-app` is generated by CloudNativePG. Fill the code PVC with deploy/push-code.sh.\\n# ingest: the stateless HTTP front of the session API. Two replicas, any node, no volume.\\n# Code delivery: an initContainer unpacks the bundle in ConfigMap `ingest-code` (deploy/push-ingest.sh builds it,\\n# about 140 KB, limit 1 MiB) into an emptyDir. Chosen over a code PVC because a ReadWriteOnce volume cannot be\\n# shared across nodes, which would pin both replicas to one node; the ConfigMap needs no pinning and a rollout\\n# is \\\"replace the ConfigMap, restart\\\". No image is pushed.\\n# Ingest never talks to the orchestrator except to proxy a stream, so it keeps accepting events while the\\n# orchestrator restarts. Its readiness depends on the database only.\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: pi-ingest\\n  labels:\\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-ingest\\nspec:\\n  replicas: 2\\n  strategy:\\n    type: RollingUpdate\\n    rollingUpdate: { maxUnavailable: 1, maxSurge: 1 }\\n  selector:\\n    matchLabels:\\n      app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-ingest\\n  template:\\n    metadata:\\n      labels:\\n        app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-ingest\\n    spec:\\n      automountServiceAccountToken: false\\n      securityContext:\\n        runAsNonRoot: true\\n        runAsUser: 1000\\n        runAsGroup: 1000\\n        fsGroup: 1000\\n        seccompProfile:\\n          type: RuntimeDefault\\n      affinity:\\n        podAntiAffinity:\\n          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:\\n            - weight: 100\\n              podAffinityTerm:\\n                topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname\\n                labelSelector:\\n                  matchLabels:\\n                    app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-ingest\\n      initContainers:\\n        - name: unpack\\n          image: node:22-bookworm-slim\\n          command: [sh, -c, \\\"mkdir -p /app/code && tar xzmf /bundle/app.tgz -C /app/code && test -f /app/code/dist/main.js\\\"]\\n          resources:\\n            requests: { cpu: 10m, memory: 32Mi }\\n            limits: { cpu: 100m, memory: 64Mi }\\n          securityContext:\\n            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\\n            capabilities:\\n              drop: [ALL]\\n          volumeMounts:\\n            - { name: bundle, mountPath: /bundle, readOnly: true }\\n            - { name: app, mountPath: /app }\\n      containers:\\n        - name: ingest\\n          image: node:22-bookworm-slim\\n          workingDir: /app/code\\n          command: [node, dist/main.js]\\n          # The default model and allowlist POST /sessions is checked against (the orchestrator reads the same).\\n          envFrom:\\n            - configMapRef: { name: pi-model-config }\\n          env:\\n            - { name: HOME, value: /tmp }\\n            - { name: PI_INGEST_PORT, value: \\\"8080\\\" }\\n            - { name: PI_ORCH_URL, value: \\\"http://pi-orchestrator:8081\\\" }\\n            - name: POD_NAME\\n              valueFrom: { fieldRef: { fieldPath: metadata.name } }\\n            # The CNPG -rw service, straight: no transaction-pooled proxy.\\n            - name: PI_DURABLE_DATABASE_URL\\n              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: pi-pg-app, key: uri } }\\n            - name: PI_API_TOKENS\\n              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: api-auth, key: tokens } }\\n          ports:\\n            - { name: http, containerPort: 8080 }\\n          readinessProbe:\\n            httpGet: { path: /readyz, port: http }\\n            periodSeconds: 5\\n          livenessProbe:\\n            httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: http }\\n            periodSeconds: 10\\n            failureThreshold: 6\\n          resources:\\n            requests: { cpu: 50m, memory: 128Mi }\\n            limits: { cpu: 250m, memory: 256Mi }\\n          securityContext:\\n            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\\n            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\\n            capabilities:\\n              drop: [ALL]\\n          volumeMounts:\\n            - { name: app, mountPath: /app, readOnly: true }\\n            - { name: tmp, mountPath: /tmp }\\n      volumes:\\n        - name: bundle\\n          configMap: { name: ingest-code }\\n        - name: app\\n          emptyDir: {}\\n        - name: tmp\\n          emptyDir: {}\\n---\\napiVersion: v1\\nkind: Service\\nmetadata:\\n  name: pi-ingest\\nspec:\\n  selector:\\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-ingest\\n  ports:\\n    - { name: http, port: 8080, targetPort: http }\\n# One replica, Recreate: the Postgres adapter allows a single owner per schema.\\n# Code comes from the orchestrator-code PVC (deploy/push-code.sh), so no image is pushed.\\n# The DB URI comes from the CNPG-generated secret pi-pg-app and is never printed.\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: pi-orchestrator\\n  labels:\\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-orchestrator\\nspec:\\n  replicas: 1\\n  strategy:\\n    type: Recreate\\n  selector:\\n    matchLabels:\\n      app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-orchestrator\\n      app.kubernetes.io/instance: pi-orchestrator\\n  template:\\n    metadata:\\n      labels:\\n        app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-orchestrator\\n        app.kubernetes.io/instance: pi-orchestrator\\n    spec:\\n      serviceAccountName: pi-orchestrator\\n      securityContext:\\n        runAsNonRoot: true\\n        runAsUser: 1000\\n        runAsGroup: 1000\\n        fsGroup: 1000\\n        seccompProfile:\\n          type: RuntimeDefault\\n      containers:\\n        - name: orchestrator\\n          image: node:22-bookworm-slim\\n          workingDir: /code/app\\n          command: [node, dist/main.js]\\n          # PI_MODEL_PROVIDER, PI_MODEL_ID, PI_MODEL_ALLOWLIST, PI_SESSION_TURN_BUDGET.\\n          envFrom:\\n            - configMapRef: { name: pi-model-config }\\n          env:\\n            - { name: HOME, value: /tmp }\\n            - { name: PI_ORCH_NAMESPACE, value: pi-durable-poc }\\n            - { name: PI_ORCH_WARMPOOL, value: pi-env-pool }\\n            - { name: PI_ORCH_PORT, value: \\\"8081\\\" }\\n            - { name: PI_DURABLE_SCHEMA, value: durable }\\n            - name: POD_NAME\\n              valueFrom: { fieldRef: { fieldPath: metadata.name } }\\n            - name: PI_DURABLE_DATABASE_URL\\n              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: pi-pg-app, key: uri } }\\n            # Master key of the per-sandbox sandboxd tokens: HMAC(master, claim). Never reaches a bridge or a sandbox.\\n            - name: PI_SANDBOX_MASTER_KEY\\n              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: sandboxd-auth, key: master } }\\n            # Same bearer tokens as ingest (create with deploy/gen-api-secret.sh): the stream endpoint\\n            # authenticates the caller again and checks the session owner.\\n            - name: PI_API_TOKENS\\n              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: api-auth, key: tokens } }\\n            # Model calls go through the OneCLI gateway with this agent token (an ExternalSecret from secret-store,\\n            # deploy/onecli-externalsecret.yaml). Optional so the scripted model runs without it. It is read from the\\n            # environment once and never logged; bridge-launcher strips it from every bridge child.\\n            - name: ONECLI_AGENT_TOKEN\\n              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: pi-onecli, key: agent-token, optional: true } }\\n            # Phase 4c: management API key for per-session OneCLI agents (repo sessions only; optional).\\n            - name: ONECLI_API_KEY\\n              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: pi-onecli-admin, key: api-key, optional: true } }\\n            - { name: PI_ONECLI_API, value: \\\"http://onecli.onecli:10256\\\" }\\n            - { name: PI_ONECLI_GATEWAY, value: \\\"http://onecli.onecli:10255\\\" }\\n            - { name: PI_ONECLI_CA_FILE, value: /etc/onecli/ca.pem }\\n            # No process-wide HTTPS_PROXY: only pi-ai's model calls use a dispatcher scoped to the gateway. NO_PROXY\\n            # is the belt and braces list (cluster, Kubernetes API, Postgres) the orchestrator also applies itself.\\n            - { name: NO_PROXY, value: \\\"localhost,127.0.0.1,.svc,.cluster.local,kubernetes.default,pi-pg-rw,10.0.0.0/8\\\" }\\n            # Test-only, both off unless set. CONTROL mounts the authenticated Phase 3 scripted-session surface\\n            # under /control. TEST_FAULTS honours payload.testFault on inbox rows, which only SQL can write\\n            # (ingest never does), so the Phase 4 suite can kill the orchestrator between submit and mark.\\n          ports:\\n            - { name: control, containerPort: 8081 }\\n          readinessProbe:\\n            httpGet: { path: /readyz, port: control }\\n            periodSeconds: 3\\n          resources:\\n            requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 256Mi }\\n            limits: { cpu: \\\"1\\\", memory: 2Gi }\\n          securityContext:\\n            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\\n            capabilities:\\n              drop: [ALL]\\n          volumeMounts:\\n            - { name: code, mountPath: /code, readOnly: true }\\n            - { name: onecli-ca, mountPath: /etc/onecli, readOnly: true }\\n            - { name: github-app, mountPath: /etc/pi-github-app, readOnly: true }\\n      volumes:\\n        # GitHub App key for minting per-repo tokens (ExternalSecret pi-github-app); optional like the CA.\\n        - name: github-app\\n          secret: { secretName: pi-github-app, optional: true }\\n        # Filled by deploy/fetch-onecli-ca.sh; optional so the scripted model starts without it.\\n        - name: onecli-ca\\n          configMap: { name: onecli-ca, optional: true }\\n        - name: code\\n          persistentVolumeClaim:\\n            claimName: orchestrator-code\\n# Topology A: the runtime container is sandboxd; pi-env must live in this image\\n# because sandboxd runs commands in its own rootfs.\\nFROM registry.k8s.io/agent-sandbox/sandboxd:v1.0.11\\n\\n# pi-env 1.5.2 linux-arm64 static binary, taken from the npm tarball by build.sh\\n# (which verifies this digest before the build).\\nARG PI_ENV_SHA256=23094f7089e3ab4410431ced346ee50944ae601ea91aa14061573d23d4e3d8c3\\nCOPY --chmod=0755 pi-env /usr/local/bin/pi-env\\nUSER root\\nRUN echo \\\"${PI_ENV_SHA256}  /usr/local/bin/pi-env\\\" | sha256sum -c -\\n# Phase 4c: repo sessions clone and push with git through the OneCLI gateway, and trust its CA on top of the system roots.\\nRUN apt-get update \\\\\\n && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends git ca-certificates \\\\\\n && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\\nUSER 1000\\n# OneCLI v2 host rules (public egress allowlist)\\n\\nWhy: OneCLI v2.20.0 refuses private, cluster and metadata targets, but it relays **any public host** for a valid agent\\ntoken (`docs/research/onecli-v2-recheck.md`, section 2). The `Default Rule: block` switch (`/v1/org/policy/default`) does\\nnot change that: it only applies to credentialed non-LLM requests. The working allowlist is ordered explicit org rules:\\n`allow <host>` for each host, then `block *`. That test returned 403 for every host not allowed and 200 with injection for\\n`openrouter.ai`.\\n\\nThis is the gateway-side control (what a leaked token can ask for). The pod-side control is the Cilium FQDN allowlist\\nin `deploy/onecli-policies/egress.yaml`; keep both lists identical.\\n\\n## The rule set\\n\\nRule shape (recheck doc): `{\\\"name\\\", \\\"action\\\": \\\"allow|block\\\", \\\"targets\\\": [{\\\"kind\\\": \\\"network\\\", \\\"hostPattern\\\"}]}`, at\\n`POST /v1/org/policy/rules`, effective immediately. No `pathPattern` or `method`: path rules are bypassable (`..`,\\n`/repositories/<id>`), so the boundary is the host. Repository scope comes from the per-session GitHub App token, not from here.\\n\\n| # | action | hostPattern | Why |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | allow | `chatgpt.com` | Codex subscription calls (credential injected) |\\n| 2 | allow | `*.chatgpt.com` | same, subdomains |\\n| 3 | allow | `auth.openai.com` | OAuth token refresh |\\n| 4 | allow | `api.openai.com` | OpenAI API |\\n| 5 | allow | `openrouter.ai` | OpenRouter models |\\n| 6 | allow | `github.com` | git clone and push over HTTPS (Phase 4c) |\\n| 7 | allow | `api.github.com` | GitHub API, installation-token mint if it goes through the gateway |\\n| 8 | allow | `codeload.github.com` | archive downloads |\\n| 9 | allow | `objects.githubusercontent.com` | release and LFS objects |\\n| 10 | allow | `registry.npmjs.org` | npm |\\n| 11 | allow | `pypi.org` | pip index |\\n| 12 | allow | `files.pythonhosted.org` | pip files |\\n| 13 | block | `*` | everything else, **created last** |\\n\\nOrder is the point: rule 13 must be created after rules 1 to 12. The script keeps that invariant (see below).\\nWhether the gateway evaluates in creation order or by specificity is UNVERIFIED beyond the recheck test (allow then block\\nbehaved as an allowlist); check with the probes below after applying.\\n\\n## Apply\\n\\n`deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh`, applied on c2 on 2026-11-16 (the org had no rules before). Dry run by default.\\n\\n```sh\\ndeploy/onecli-host-rules.sh            # prints the plan, makes no request\\ndeploy/onecli-host-rules.sh --apply    # needs the admin key\\n```\\n\\n- Admin key: Secret `secret-store/pi-onecli-admin`, key `api-key`, read with `kubectl` (or `ONECLI_API_KEY`). It goes to\\n  curl on stdin and is never printed. This is the admin key the plan says must never live in the orchestrator: run the\\n  script from an operator machine only.\\n- `ONECLI_URL` (required): the OneCLI API base, e.g. `https://onecli.example.com`. Use `http://localhost:10256` with\\n  `kubectl -n onecli port-forward svc/onecli 10256` if the Cilium ingress policy has closed the route.\\n- Idempotent by rule name (`pi-allow-<host>`, `pi-block-all`). Adding a host later: add it to `hosts` in the script and to\\n  `egress.yaml`, rerun; the script deletes `pi-block-all` and recreates it last. There is a short window with no block\\n  rule (the gateway relays public hosts as before), so do it when no session is running.\\n- The list response is a bare array (seen on 2026-11-16, empty before the first apply). UNVERIFIED: `DELETE\\n  /v1/org/policy/rules/<id>` and the id field (not exercised yet).\\n\\n## Verify after applying\\n\\nFrom a pod that holds an agent token (the orchestrator has one; do not print it), through `:10255`:\\n\\n- `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/key` returns 200 with the secret injected.\\n- `https://example.com` and `https://api.ipify.org` return 403.\\n- `https://github.com/<public repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack` returns 200 (a private repo needs the session token).\\n\\n## Result (2026-11-16)\\n\\nProbes from the orchestrator pod through `:10255` with its agent token (not printed), right after `--apply`:\\n\\n| Target | Status |\\n|---|---|\\n| `https://example.com/` | 403 |\\n| `https://api.ipify.org/` | 403 |\\n| `https://github.com/<owner>/<repo-a>.git/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack` | 200 |\\n| `https://registry.npmjs.org/left-pad` | 200 |\\n| `https://chatgpt.com/` | 200 |\\n\\nThe Cilium FQDN policy on the OneCLI pod (argo-c2 `applications/onecli`) is the second layer: a host allowed here but\\nmissing there fails to connect.\\n\\nSandboxes still reach public hosts directly (`sandbox-allow-internet-egress`, decision 2026-11-15), so this list only\\nlimits what goes through the gateway: model calls and git with injected credentials. Routing sandbox traffic through the\\ngateway too would need `HTTPS_PROXY` in the sandbox, the OneCLI CA in every tool, and the package hosts each session\\nneeds on this list. It is not done; revisit before real use with untrusted code.\\n\\n## Rollback\\n\\nList rules with `GET /v1/org/policy/rules` and delete `pi-block-all` first (restores relaying of public hosts as in\\nv2.20.0 defaults), then the `pi-allow-*` rules if wanted. Deleting only the block rule is enough to undo the restriction.\\n    87\\tcleanup() {\\n    88\\t  local id\\n    89\\t  while read -r id; do\\n    90\\t    [[ -n $id ]] || continue\\n    91\\t    api alice DELETE \\\"/sessions/$id\\\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\\n    92\\t  done <\\\"$sessions_file\\\"\\n    93\\t  # The delete is asynchronous (the orchestrator removes the sandbox first): wait until every session is `deleted`\\n    94\\t  # (bounded), then drop our rows. Dropping them earlier would orphan a claim the delete had not reached yet.\\n    95\\t  local list deadline left=1\\n    96\\t  list=$(grep -v '^$' \\\"$sessions_file\\\" | sed \\\"s/.*/'&'/\\\" | paste -sd, -)\\n    97\\t  deadline=$(($(now) + 180))\\n    98\\t  while [[ -n $list && $left -ne 0 && $(now) -lt $deadline ]]; do\\n    99\\t    left=$(sql \\\"select count(*) from inbox.sessions where status <> 'deleted' and id in ($list)\\\" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ' || echo 1)\\n   100\\t    [[ $left == 0 ]] || sleep 3\\n   101\\t  done\\n   102\\t  while read -r id; do\\n   103\\t    [[ -n $id ]] || continue\\n   104\\t    sql \\\"delete from inbox.events where session_id='$id'; delete from inbox.sessions where id='$id'\\\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\\n   105\\t  done <\\\"$sessions_file\\\"\\n   106\\t  rm -rf \\\"$work\\\"\\n   107\\t}\\n    15\\tk() { kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" \\\"$@\\\"; }\\n    16\\ttrap 'rm -rf \\\"$work\\\"; k delete pod orchestrator-code-loader --ignore-not-found --wait=false >/dev/null 2>&1 || true' EXIT\\n    17\\t\\n    18\\t(cd \\\"$root\\\" && pnpm install --frozen-lockfile >/dev/null && pnpm --filter \\\"@pi-durable-k8s/orchestrator...\\\" build >/dev/null)\\n    19\\t(cd \\\"$root\\\" && pnpm --filter @pi-durable-k8s/orchestrator deploy --prod \\\"$work/app\\\" >/dev/null)\\n    20\\tCOPYFILE_DISABLE=1 tar --no-xattrs -czf \\\"$work/app.tgz\\\" -C \\\"$work/app\\\" .\\n    21\\t\\n    22\\tk apply -f \\\"$here/orchestrator-code-pvc.yaml\\\" >/dev/null\\n    23\\tfor d in \\\"${deployments[@]}\\\"; do\\n    24\\t  k get deployment \\\"$d\\\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && k scale deployment \\\"$d\\\" --replicas=0 >/dev/null\\n    25\\tdone\\n    26\\tfor d in \\\"${deployments[@]}\\\"; do\\n    27\\t  k wait --for=delete pod -l \\\"app.kubernetes.io/instance=$d\\\" --timeout=120s >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\\n    28\\tdone\\n    29\\t\\n    30\\tk delete pod orchestrator-code-loader --ignore-not-found --wait=true >/dev/null\\n    31\\tk apply -f - >/dev/null <<EOF\\n    32\\tapiVersion: v1\\n    33\\tkind: Pod\\n    34\\tmetadata:\\n    35\\t  name: orchestrator-code-loader\\n    36\\tspec:\\n    37\\t  restartPolicy: Never\\n    38\\t  securityContext: { runAsNonRoot: true, runAsUser: 1000, runAsGroup: 1000, fsGroup: 1000, seccompProfile: { type: RuntimeDefault } }\\n    39\\t  containers:\\n    40\\t    - name: loader\\n    41\\t      image: node:22-bookworm-slim\\n    42\\t      command: [sleep, \\\"600\\\"]\\n    43\\t      securityContext: { allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, capabilities: { drop: [ALL] } }\\n    44\\t      resources:\\n    45\\t        requests: { cpu: 10m, memory: 32Mi }\\n    46\\t        limits: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi }\\n    47\\t      volumeMounts: [{ name: code, mountPath: /code }]\\n    48\\t  volumes:\\n    49\\t    - name: code\\n    50\\t      persistentVolumeClaim: { claimName: orchestrator-code }\\n    51\\tEOF\\n    52\\tk wait --for=condition=Ready pod/orchestrator-code-loader --timeout=240s >/dev/null\\n    53\\tk cp \\\"$work/app.tgz\\\" orchestrator-code-loader:/tmp/app.tgz >/dev/null\\n    54\\tk exec orchestrator-code-loader -- sh -c 'rm -rf /code/app && mkdir -p /code/app && tar xzf /tmp/app.tgz -C /code/app && ls /code/app/dist/main.js >/dev/null'\\n    55\\tk delete pod orchestrator-code-loader --wait=true >/dev/null\\n    56\\t\\n    57\\tfor d in \\\"${deployments[@]}\\\"; do\\n    58\\t  k get deployment \\\"$d\\\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && k scale deployment \\\"$d\\\" --replicas=1 >/dev/null\\n    59\\tdone\\n     1\\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n     2\\t# Create secret api-auth in pi-durable-poc: key `tokens` holds one `owner:token` line per API owner.\\n     3\\t# Never commit or print its content. Idempotent: an existing secret is left alone (set ROTATE=1 to replace it;\\n     4\\t# running orchestrator and ingest pods pick the new value up on their next restart).\\n     5\\t# Owners: alice and bob, so the e2e suite can test \\\"wrong owner\\\". Real owners replace these when OIDC lands.\\n     6\\tset -euo pipefail\\n     7\\tctx=${KUBE_CONTEXT:-} # empty when run in-cluster (e2e driver Job): ServiceAccount config\\n     8\\tns=${NAMESPACE:-pi-durable-poc}\\n     9\\tif [[ ${ROTATE:-0} != 1 ]] && kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" get secret api-auth >/dev/null 2>&1; then\\n    10\\t  echo \\\"secret api-auth already exists (ROTATE=1 to replace)\\\"\\n    11\\t  exit 0\\n    12\\tfi\\n    13\\ttokens=$(printf 'alice:%s\\\\nbob:%s\\\\n' \\\"$(openssl rand -hex 32)\\\" \\\"$(openssl rand -hex 32)\\\")\\n    14\\tkubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" create secret generic api-auth \\\\\\n    15\\t  --from-literal=tokens=\\\"$tokens\\\" \\\\\\n    16\\t  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\\\"$ctx\\\"} -n \\\"$ns\\\" apply -f - >/dev/null\\n    17\\techo \\\"secret api-auth created\\\"\\n    13\\texists=0\\n    14\\tif k get secret sandboxd-auth >/dev/null 2>&1; then\\n    15\\t  exists=1\\n    16\\t  if [[ -n $(k get secret sandboxd-auth -o jsonpath='{.data.master}') && ${ROTATE:-0} != 1 ]]; then\\n    17\\t    echo \\\"sandboxd-auth: master exists, kept (ROTATE=1 to replace)\\\"\\n    18\\t    exit 0\\n    19\\t  fi\\n    20\\tfi\\n    21\\tmaster=$(openssl rand -hex 32)\\n    22\\tmanifest=$(k create secret generic sandboxd-auth --from-literal=master=\\\"$master\\\" --dry-run=client -o yaml)\\n    23\\t# replace swaps the whole data map, which also drops the legacy keys.\\n    24\\tif [[ $exists == 1 ]]; then k replace -f - <<<\\\"$manifest\\\" >/dev/null; else k create -f - <<<\\\"$manifest\\\" >/dev/null; fi\\n    25\\techo \\\"sandboxd-auth: master written\\\"\\n\",\"isError\":false}]}\n./deploy/ingest.yaml:68:            - { name: PI_INGEST_PORT, value: \"8080\" }\n./deploy/ingest.yaml:69:            - { name: PI_ORCH_URL, value: \"http://pi-orchestrator:8081\" }\n./packages/ingest/src/config.ts:22:  const port = Number(env.PI_INGEST_PORT ?? \"8080\");\n./packages/ingest/src/config.ts:24:    throw new Error(\"PI_INGEST_PORT is invalid\");\n./packages/ingest/src/config.ts:29:    orchestratorUrl: env.PI_ORCH_URL ?? \"http://pi-orchestrator:8081\",\n./packages/orchestrator/src/config.ts:9:/** Environment contract of the orchestrator. Secrets (database URL, sandboxd token, API tokens) are env-only. */\n# ingest: the stateless HTTP front of the session API. Two replicas, any node, no volume.\n# Code delivery: an initContainer unpacks the bundle in ConfigMap `ingest-code` (deploy/push-ingest.sh builds it,\n# about 140 KB, limit 1 MiB) into an emptyDir. Chosen over a code PVC because a ReadWriteOnce volume cannot be\n# shared across nodes, which would pin both replicas to one node; the ConfigMap needs no pinning and a rollout\n# is \"replace the ConfigMap, restart\". No image is pushed.\n# Ingest never talks to the orchestrator except to proxy a stream, so it keeps accepting events while the\n# orchestrator restarts. Its readiness depends on the database only.\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: pi-ingest\n  labels:\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-ingest\nspec:\n  replicas: 2\n  strategy:\n    type: RollingUpdate\n    rollingUpdate: { maxUnavailable: 1, maxSurge: 1 }\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-ingest\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-ingest\n    spec:\n      automountServiceAccountToken: false\n      securityContext:\n        runAsNonRoot: true\n        runAsUser: 1000\n        runAsGroup: 1000\n        fsGroup: 1000\n        seccompProfile:\n          type: RuntimeDefault\n      affinity:\n        podAntiAffinity:\n          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:\n            - weight: 100\n              podAffinityTerm:\n                topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname\n                labelSelector:\n                  matchLabels:\n                    app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-ingest\n      initContainers:\n        - name: unpack\n          image: node:22-bookworm-slim\n          command: [sh, -c, \"mkdir -p /app/code && tar xzmf /bundle/app.tgz -C /app/code && test -f /app/code/dist/main.js\"]\n          resources:\n            requests: { cpu: 10m, memory: 32Mi }\n            limits: { cpu: 100m, memory: 64Mi }\n          securityContext:\n            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n            capabilities:\n              drop: [ALL]\n          volumeMounts:\n            - { name: bundle, mountPath: /bundle, readOnly: true }\n            - { name: app, mountPath: /app }\n      containers:\n        - name: ingest\n          image: node:22-bookworm-slim\n          workingDir: /app/code\n          command: [node, dist/main.js]\n          # The default model and allowlist POST /sessions is checked against (the orchestrator reads the same).\n          envFrom:\n            - configMapRef: { name: pi-model-config }\n          env:\n            - { name: HOME, value: /tmp }\n            - { name: PI_INGEST_PORT, value: \"8080\" }\n            - { name: PI_ORCH_URL, value: \"http://pi-orchestrator:8081\" }\n            - name: POD_NAME\n              valueFrom: { fieldRef: { fieldPath: metadata.name } }\n            # The CNPG -rw service, straight: no transaction-pooled proxy.\n            - name: PI_DURABLE_DATABASE_URL\n              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: pi-pg-app, key: uri } }\n            - name: PI_API_TOKENS\n              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: api-auth, key: tokens } }\n          ports:\n            - { name: http, containerPort: 8080 }\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet: { path: /readyz, port: http }\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: http }\n            periodSeconds: 10\n            failureThreshold: 6\n          resources:\n            requests: { cpu: 50m, memory: 128Mi }\n            limits: { cpu: 250m, memory: 256Mi }\n          securityContext:\n            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\n            capabilities:\n              drop: [ALL]\n          volumeMounts:\n            - { name: app, mountPath: /app, readOnly: true }\n            - { name: tmp, mountPath: /tmp }\n      volumes:\n        - name: bundle\n          configMap: { name: ingest-code }\n        - name: app\n          emptyDir: {}\n        - name: tmp\n          emptyDir: {}\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: pi-ingest\nspec:\n  selector:\nimport { loadModelPolicy } from \"@pi-durable-k8s/session-core\";\nimport type { ModelPolicy } from \"@pi-durable-k8s/session-core\";\n\n/** Environment contract of ingest. The database URL and the API tokens are secrets: env-only, never logged. */\nexport interface IngestConfig {\n  databaseUrl: string;\n  /** `owner:token` entries, see session-core `tokenAuthenticator`. */\n  apiTokens: string;\n  /** Base URL of the orchestrator's stream endpoint. */\n  orchestratorUrl: string;\n  port: number;\n  instance: string;\n  /** Default model and allowlist for POST /sessions (same variables as the orchestrator, from one ConfigMap). */\n  models: ModelPolicy;\n}\n\nexport function loadConfig(env: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>): IngestConfig {\n  const databaseUrl = env.PI_DURABLE_DATABASE_URL;\n  if (!databaseUrl) throw new Error(\"PI_DURABLE_DATABASE_URL is required\");\n  const apiTokens = env.PI_API_TOKENS;\n  if (!apiTokens) throw new Error(\"PI_API_TOKENS is required\");\n  const port = Number(env.PI_INGEST_PORT ?? \"8080\");\n  if (!Number.isInteger(port) || port < 0 || port > 65535) {\n    throw new Error(\"PI_INGEST_PORT is invalid\");\n  }\n  return {\n    databaseUrl,\n    apiTokens,\n    orchestratorUrl: env.PI_ORCH_URL ?? \"http://pi-orchestrator:8081\",\n    port,\n    instance: env.POD_NAME ?? `ingest-${String(process.pid)}`,\n    models: loadModelPolicy(env),\n  };\n}\n<harness>\n[info] Full output: /tmp/tmp-jPDkMF/pi-output-1864d1f6-e8af-4d13-8b2e-9d3c1d34fc04.log\n[warn] Output truncated to its end: 68 lines, 256732 bytes dropped\n</harness>","isError":false}]}
ENTRY 11941 {"id":11941,"kind":"pi.assistant","messages":[{"role":"assistant","text":"## Findings\n### 1. Empty port configuration binds an unpredictable port\n- Severity: low\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/ingest/src/config.ts:22\n- What happens: If `PI_INGEST_PORT` is set to an empty or whitespace-only string, `Number()` converts it to `0`, which passes validation. The server then listens on an OS-assigned port rather than the configured service and probe port, making the pod unreachable.\n- Evidence: `const port = Number(env.PI_INGEST_PORT ?? \"8080\");`\n- Fix idea: Reject empty and whitespace-only values before numeric conversion, or treat them as absent and use the default.\n\n### 2. Unsupported orchestrator URLs are accepted at startup\n- Severity: low\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/ingest/src/config.ts:29\n- What happens: An empty, malformed, or HTTPS `PI_ORCH_URL` is accepted, so ingest can become ready while every stream request fails with a 500. The stream proxy uses `node:http` and cannot request an HTTPS URL.\n- Evidence: `orchestratorUrl: env.PI_ORCH_URL ?? \"http://pi-orchestrator:8081\"`\n- Fix idea: Validate the URL and its supported protocol during configuration loading. If HTTPS endpoints are intended to work, select the matching HTTP or HTTPS client in the proxy.\n\nEND-OF-REPORT"}]}
DONE
